// Title: UAC Bypass Via Wsreset
// Sigma ID: 6ea3bf32-9680-422d-9f50-e90716b12a66
// Status: test | Level: high
// Author: oscd.community, Dmitry Uchakin
// Date: 2020-10-07 | Modified: 2021-11-27
// ATT&CK: privilege-escalation, T1548.002
// Description: Unfixed method for UAC bypass from Windows 10. WSReset.exe file associated with the Windows
//   Store. It will run a binary file contained in a low-privilege registry.
// False positives:
//   - Unknown
// References:
//   - https://www.bleepingcomputer.com/news/security/trickbot-uses-a-new-windows-10-uac-bypass-to-launch-quietly
//   - https://lolbas-project.github.io/lolbas/Binaries/Wsreset
// Sigma rule: https://github.com/SigmaHQ/sigma/blob/05d1031f537e830b76da53bae2400c5814c89165/rules/windows/registry/registry_event/registry_event_bypass_via_wsreset.yml
// License: Detection Rule License (DRL) 1.1 - https://github.com/SigmaHQ/Detection-Rule-License
// Converted by sigma2kql (target: microsoft_xdr, pySigma 1.5.1, pySigma-backend-kusto 1.0.1)
// Validation: passed (Kusto.Language 12.4.1, live Defender schema)
// Machine-generated and not tested against real telemetry. Verify before use as a detection.

DeviceRegistryEvents
| where RegistryKey endswith "\\AppX82a6gwre4fdg3bt635tn5ctqjf8msdd2\\Shell\\open\\command"
